        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "What is the value of $\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}{3\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's set $u=\\ln\\left(\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)\\right).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,1\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}{3\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =\\frac{4}{9}\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =\\frac{4}{9}\\int_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}}\\right)|_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left({\\frac{{u}^{2}}{3}}\\right)|_{0}^{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
